**New Evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A Financing Agreement signed between the FDRE Ministry of Finance and the World Bank","publisher":"Ministry of Finance Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-05-16","url":"https://www.mofed.gov.et/blog/a-financing-agreement-signed-between-the-fdre-ministry-of-finance-and-the-world-bank/","claim":"The ministry said a US$300 million grant was signed to support reconstruction and recovery of conflict-affected areas, including restoring education, health and water services and special support to survivors of gender-based violence.","locator":"article body, lines 82-84","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct government announcement with explicit date, amount, and program purpose; ruler attribution is strong because it is a federal ministry action under the prime minister's cabinet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and explicitly about conflict-affected welfare services in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct federal policy and financing choice, but delivery also depended on World Bank funding and third-party implementation in high-conflict areas.","contrary_evidence":["The announcement is a financing commitment, not proof of completed delivery in 2022.","Conflict conditions limited access and execution in several targeted regions."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"House Approves Over 786 Billion Birr Budget for Ethiopian FY","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-07-07","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_37020","claim":"Ethiopia's 2022 federal budget bill was described as focusing on debt payment, humanitarian assistance, rehabilitation and reconstruction, poverty reduction, support for displaced people, and repairing damaged infrastructure and services.","locator":"article body, lines 47-55","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"National news agency report on a parliamentary budget decision, with concrete allocations and priorities, but it remains a government-facing source and does not prove implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within July 2022 and specific to the target fiscal year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the federal executive and parliament, but the article reports stated priorities rather than realized outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["Budget intent does not establish spent funds or service delivery.","The same year still saw major conflict, drought, and inflation pressures."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vulnerable Ethiopians to Benefit from $745 Million in Grants for Improved Access to Health Services and Flood Management Projects","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-13","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/14/vulnerable-ethiopians-to-benefit-from-745-million-in-grants-for-improved-access-to-health-services-and-flood-management","claim":"The World Bank said two grants would improve essential and equitable health care, including nutrition services, for women and children in conflict-affected areas, and would support flood resilience and early-warning systems for poor communities.","locator":"lines 27-35","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank press release with clear numeric funding, target population, and implementation details, though the agency is describing its own support rather than sole-government action.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Explicitly dated December 2022 and tied to conditions in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material, because it reflects government-Bank partnership and targeted implementation through conflict-affected areas; not sole ruler control.","contrary_evidence":["These are donor-financed projects, so credit is shared and implementation was still pending.","The release itself says millions remained without adequate health access."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia conducts a measles vaccination campaign to respond to an outbreak in three regions","publisher":"World Health Organization Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-04-21","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/ethiopia/news/ethiopia-conducts-measles-vaccination-campaign-respond-outbreak-three-regions","claim":"WHO reported that Ethiopia ran a non-selective measles vaccination campaign in February-March 2022 targeting more than half a million children in three regions after a risk assessment identified more than 650,000 children at risk.","locator":"lines 68-72","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO field report with program design, target population, and implementation details from national and regional health bodies.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the national public-health system and its coordination bodies, with WHO/UNICEF support; not proof of personal presidential control but relevant to ruler-period service response.","contrary_evidence":["The outbreak itself shows persistent health-system vulnerability.","Coverage and service quality in other regions remained constrained by drought and conflict."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Empowering Ethiopian Women for Climate Resilience through the Urban Productive Safety Net","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2022/03/10/empowering-ethiopian-women-for-climate-resilience-through-the-urban-productive-safety-net","claim":"The World Bank said Ethiopia's urban productive safety net gave vulnerable households, especially women, employment, direct cash transfers, community health insurance, and other basic social services, with clear rules and operational procedures institutionalizing inclusiveness.","locator":"lines 30-32 and 56-57","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank feature with explicit program design, beneficiary mix, and institutionalization details; still a bank-supported program rather than a sole ruler initiative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and describes ongoing program design in the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant, because it reflects a government-backed safety-net architecture under Abiy's administration rather than a purely donor standalone effort.","contrary_evidence":["Urban program reach was limited relative to national need.","The article is a World Bank feature, so positive framing may overstate government ownership."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fostering inclusive growth and realizing peace dividends in Ethiopia","publisher":"World Bank Blogs","publication_date":"2023-03-22","url":"https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/nasikiliza/fostering-inclusive-growth-and-realizing-peace-dividends-ethiopia","claim":"The World Bank said that in 2022 its support in Ethiopia reached nearly 8 million people with social safety nets, 5.8 million children and lactating mothers in conflict areas with health and nutrition services, 24 million students with improved educational supplies and rehabilitated facilities, and 150,000 people with access to safe water.","locator":"lines 43-50","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank summary of 2022 outputs with large quantified service-delivery claims, but it aggregates Bank-supported activities and is not a ruler-only attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Claims are explicitly about 2022 outputs, though published later as a retrospective blog.","ruler_attribution":"Partial and indirect, because the services were delivered through World Bank-supported programs and partners rather than by Abiy personally.","contrary_evidence":["Aggregated program counts can mask regional gaps and overlap across beneficiaries.","The blog is retrospective and uses Bank framing, so it needs corroboration from independent or official sources."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conflict, climate and soaring food prices push Ethiopia further into hunger while WFP funding runs out","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-06-23","url":"https://www.wfp.org/news/conflict-climate-and-soaring-food-prices-push-ethiopia-further-hunger-while-wfp-funding-runs","claim":"WFP warned that more than 20 million people in Ethiopia faced hunger, including more than 13 million in northern conflict zones needing food assistance, even as humanitarian supplies began flowing after the government's humanitarian truce.","locator":"lines 262-267","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WFP emergency reporting with specific numbers and a contemporaneous description of aid flow and remaining shortfalls.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and squarely about the target year's humanitarian crisis.","ruler_attribution":"Strong on crisis context and partial relief after a government truce, but the underlying hunger is not solely attributable to Abiy because conflict, drought, and global prices were also major drivers.","contrary_evidence":["The article also says food and humanitarian supplies were flowing after the government's truce.","Humanitarian need remained so large that WFP funding was still running out."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Appeal - Horn of Africa","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.who.int/emergencies/funding/health-emergency-appeals/2022/2023/2023-appeals/appeal-horn-of-africa","claim":"WHO said Ethiopia's routine immunization coverage remained below target because of conflict, the COVID-19 pandemic and displacement, while drought in 2022 affected at least 17 million people and disrupted routine health services.","locator":"lines 247 and 305-306","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO emergency appeal synthesizes current operational and epidemiological data, with explicit Ethiopia-specific figures and causal language.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly references 2022 conditions and the emergency response environment.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual, because the statement attributes the harm to conflict, pandemic and displacement rather than to a single national decision-maker.","contrary_evidence":["WHO also describes response activities such as outbreak campaigns and outreach services.","The appeal is regional, so Ethiopia-specific attribution is present but not exhaustive."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"EU Provides 38 Million Euros for Conflict-affected Health, Education Sectors in Ethiopia","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-11-10","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_40043","claim":"ENA reported that more than 3,000 health facilities were non-functional and nearly 700,000 children had missed routine vaccinations in northern Ethiopia, prompting an EU package to rehabilitate health facilities and rebuild schools in conflict-affected regions.","locator":"lines 55-66","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Ethiopian news report with concrete sector counts and program details, but it relays a joint press announcement and may inherit official framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and focused on service collapse in the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but important, because it indicates the state of national service failure and the need for external rehabilitation under Abiy's administration.","contrary_evidence":["The funding comes from the EU rather than the Ethiopian state.","The article highlights damage and service loss more than government performance."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Green solutions","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-09","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/reports/green-solutions","claim":"UNICEF said drought in southern and north-eastern Ethiopia meant 17 million people needed lifesaving assistance and that UNICEF and partners were investing in climate-resilient water systems so children could access clean water and hygiene services during droughts.","locator":"highlights, lines 56-58","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country report gives a direct, quantified description of crisis need and response, but it is a program-facing advocacy document rather than an independent audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 drought conditions and 2022 programming.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual and partner-driven, not a clean ruler-only action, but relevant to the government's welfare and crisis-response environment.","contrary_evidence":["The report itself says the drought was severe and children were going hungry and thirsty.","It reflects UNICEF and partner investment more than Abiy's personal policy choices."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"End Open Defecation","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-04","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/reports/end-open-defecation","claim":"UNICEF said poor sanitation coverage in Ethiopia was still driven by lack of prioritization and earmarked funding, and that open defecation continued to have devastating consequences for public health and child mortality.","locator":"highlights, lines 56-57","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country flagship report clearly states the problem mechanism, but it is a program advocacy source and does not by itself establish ruler responsibility.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and describing 2022 sanitation conditions and policy constraints.","ruler_attribution":"Mainly structural and sectoral, not a direct Abiy-specific decision claim; useful as a negative baseline check rather than a direct attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The report acknowledges Ethiopia had made significant progress.","It is a baseline/problem statement rather than evidence of specific national action or inaction in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.8"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia | Global Education Monitoring Report","publisher":"UNESCO Global Education Monitoring Report","publication_date":"2026","url":"https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en/2026-gem-report-country-case-studies/ethiopia?hub=1074","claim":"UNESCO's Ethiopia case study says conflict caused enrolment to fall by 24 percent between 2020 and 2023 and that education spending had fallen to 3.7 percent of GDP in 2022, marking a reversal in educational development.","locator":"lines 481-485","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Later synthesized education case study with explicit source references and national trend data, but it is retrospective and partly based on later Ministry of Education data.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospectively describes the 2022 education trajectory and immediately adjacent years.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect and partially confounded by war and federal-regional conflict; useful for trajectory but not a clean ruler-only causal attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that Ethiopia had invested heavily in education over the long run.","Because it is retrospective, the causal story is not fully attributable to Abiy alone."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across UNICEF, WHO, WFP, World Bank, ENA, UNESCO, UNHCR, and World Bank archive pages, then kept 11 new source-claim units plus 3 reused items.
- Duplicates removed: the UNICEF Ethiopia annual report appeared in several search hits; the May 16 Ministry of Finance agreement duplicated the April 12 World Bank conflict-affected-communities project; the broader World Bank and WFP country pages repeated claims already captured in the targeted 2022 articles.
- Blocked or inaccessible items removed: UNHCR node and situation pages returned 402/404, and some ecoi mirrors exposed only index shells without the underlying report text.
- Out-of-period or low-value items removed: 2024-2026 digital ID, PSNP-6, and education-video pages were either too far outside 2022 or too generic to improve ruler-specific attribution.
- I also treated some sources as context only when they were useful for baseline or constraint setting but did not add clean ruler attribution, especially sanitation, drought, and retrospective education trend sources.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on Abiy Ahmed's own welfare priorities, as distinct from donor-supported programs and inherited safety nets, remains thin.
- Better 2022 household outcome data would help, especially malnutrition, learning loss, sanitation access, and region-by-region coverage for vulnerable groups.
- More direct execution evidence would help for budget outturns, service quality, and whether the 2022 priorities in the budget and financing agreements were actually implemented on time and at scale.
